> How can I use the Zotero API?
**assistant**:
> Using the Zotero API allows you to interact programmatically with your Zotero library, enabling you to retrieve, manage, and manipulate your references. Here’s a step-by-step guide on how to use the Zotero API:
> 
> ### Step 1: Create a Zotero API Key
> 1. **Log in to Zotero**: Go to [Zotero.org](https://www.zotero.org/) and log into your account.
> 2. **Go to Settings**: Click on your username and select **Settings**.
> 3. **API Keys**: Navigate to the **API** tab.
> 4. **Create a New Key**: Click on **Create New Private Key**. Set permissions appropriately (e.g., “Read” access if you only want to retrieve data).
> 5. **Copy Your Key**: Make sure to save this API key securely, as you will use it for authentication.
> 
> ### Step 2: Understand the Basic API Endpoint Structure
> The basic structure of a Zotero API URL looks like this:
> ```
> https://api.zotero.org/users/{userID}/items
> ```
> - **`{userID}`**: Replace with your Zotero user ID, which you can find in the API settings.
> 
> ### Step 3: Make API Requests
> You can interact with the API using any programming language that can make HTTP requests. Below is an example using Python with the `requests` library.
> 
> #### Example: Retrieve Your Zotero Items
> ```python
> import requests
> 
> # Replace with your user ID and API key
> user_id = 'YOUR_USER_ID'
> api_key = 'YOUR_API_KEY'
> 
> # Set up headers for authentication
> headers = {
>     'Zotero-API-Key': api_key
> }
> 
> # URL for retrieving items
> url = f'https://api.zotero.org/users/{user_id}/items'
> 
> # Make the GET request
> response = requests.get(url, headers=headers)
> 
> # Check if the request was successful
> if response.status_code == 200:
>     items = response.json()
>     for item in items:
>         print(item['title'])  # Or any other item fields you want
> else:
>     print(f'Error: {response.status_code}')
> ```
> 
> ### Step 4: Navigate Through Collections and Folders
> If you want to get references along with their collection information, you can use the following endpoints:
> 
> 1. **Collections**:
>    - To get collections: `https://api.zotero.org/users/{userID}/collections`
> 2. **Items in a Collection**:
>    - To get items in a specific collection: `https://api.zotero.org/users/{userID}/collections/{collectionKey}/items`
> 
> ### Step 5: Parse and Utilize the Data
> Once you retrieve the data, you can process it according to your needs. For example, if you tagged items based on their collection, you can organize or export them.
> 
> ### Additional Resources
> - **Zotero API Documentation**: Read the [official documentation](https://www.zotero.org/support/dev/web_api/v3/start) for details on endpoints, parameters, and data structures.
> - **Community**: Engage with the [Zotero Forums](https://forums.zotero.org/) for help and examples from other users.
> 
> Feel free to ask if you need more detailed examples or help with a specific programming language!
